./xmodel/max12-912-order3-nu-q8-infinity-primitivity-review-claude-20260825.md:43:**Decisive evidence.** `Z!=0` is not implicit: infinity report section 2 derives `r8` nonconstant from `9*r8'=j/u`, `j!=0`, `u!=0`, hence `S=r8^9` and `Z=S/nu^10` nonzero and nonconstant — exactly the terminal review's section 10 discharge route (i). Unit inventory, each traced to a reviewed parent: `pi0=nu/n0` nonzero (gate `n0` unit, fibre `nu!=0`); `S0=rho0^9` and `S1` units (leaf4 claim 3 gcd certificates over `E`, valid at all eight contacts by `gcd(Q8,Q8')=1`); `Z0=S0/nu^10!=0`; `lambda^3=Z0` so `lambda!=0` and finite (balanced degrees, terminal review section 7); `3*lambda^2!=0` in characteristic zero. Orders: `ord_infinity(pi∘phi)=0` since `pi(q_Q8)=pi0` is a nonzero finite value, so `ord_infinity(theta)=2a` with `a=ord_infinity(a0)`, `a0 in C(x)` (Kummer-fixed). Positivity: `theta(q_Q8)=0` and `phi(infinity)=q_Q8` give `2a>0`; finiteness: `a0 ≡ 0` would force the quotient image into the `w=0` locus, which meets `C_Q8` in finitely many points, contradicting surjectivity. The formal expansion `S-S0=S1*theta+O(theta^2)` pulls back functorially along the completed local map at `infinity`, `S1` is a nonzero constant, and the tail has order `>=4a`, so `ord_infinity(Z-Z0)=2a`, which is (4.2). Cubing: `Z-Z0=(T-lambda)(T^2+lambda*T+lambda^2)` and the cofactor takes the value `3*lambda^2!=0` at `infinity`, so `e_pass=ord_infinity(T-lambda)=2a`, even and `>=2` — the parity of `e_pass` traces to the parity-involution square `theta=a0^2/pi`, not to the Kummer cube; the report keeps `e_pass` and the Kummer order three explicitly distinct. Decisive cross-check: (4.5) gives `deg(h)=3*(e_pass+1)=3*(2a+1)`, which coincides exactly with the normalization-jet review section 7 independently derived necessary condition `deg(h)=3*(2e+1)` with `e=a`. Two independent routes agree.
./xmodel/max12-912-order3-nu-q8-infinity-primitivity-review-claude-20260825.md:67:**Decisive evidence.** The displayed `Q8(v)` is coefficient-for-coefficient the erratum octic reconfirmed by Gaussian elimination in the formal-branch review, and `Q8_Z` in `replay.py` is its exact negation (leading term `+999`). Content: `999=3^3*37`, `296=2^3*37`, `gcd(37,24)=1`, so content one — hand-verified. Good primes: `999 ≡ 5 (mod 7)` and `999 ≡ 45 (mod 53)`, both nonzero. Hand reduction mod 7 gives ascending `[4,5,6,3,2,5,3,6,5]`; multiplying by `3=5^{-1}` yields the monic `[5,1,4,2,6,1,2,4,1]` — equal to the frozen certificate and, decisively, to the independent mod-7 certificate hand-verified inside the normalization-jet review: two independent artifacts certify the same irreducibility. Hand reduction mod 53 gives ascending `[29,22,42,24,47,21,20,2,45]`; multiplying by `33=45^{-1}` yields `[3,37,8,50,14,4,24,13,1]`, the frozen monic. Hand evaluation with `13^2=10, 13^3=24, 13^4=47, 13^5=28, 13^6=46, 13^7=15, 13^8=36` gives `f(13) ≡ 212 ≡ 0`; synthetic division by `v-13` reproduces the frozen degree-seven quotient `[12,47,3,29,46,44,26,1]` with zero remainder; and `F7(13) ≡ 177 ≡ 18 != 0`, so given `F7` irreducible (frozen), `13` is simple, the pattern is exactly `[1,7]`, and squarefreeness mod 53 follows without trusting the separate gcd. Rabin logic is correct at both degrees: for `n=8` the sole prime divisor is 2, so `gcd(f, v^(p^4)-v)=1` plus `v^(p^8) ≡ v` is necessary and sufficient; for `n=7` the sole prime divisor is 7, giving `gcd(F7, v^53-v)=1` plus `v^(53^7) ≡ v`. Irreducible mod 7 with degree preserved and content one lifts to irreducibility over `Z`, hence `Q`, by Gauss: transitivity. Dedekind at a non-monic `f` with `p` prime to `lc(f)`: pass to the monicization `lc^(n-1)*f(w/lc)`, whose mod-`p` factor pattern equals `f`'s under the invertible substitution `w -> w/lc` and whose roots are the `lc`-scalings of `f`'s roots, so the Frobenius cycle types `[8]` at 7 and `[1,7]` at 53 act on the eight roots as stated. Primitivity: a nontrivial block in a transitive degree-eight action has size 2 or 4 (block size divides the degree); the full stabilizers of such partitions in `S8` are `S2 wr S4` of order `2^4*4!=384=2^7*3` and `S4 wr S2` of order `(4!)^2*2=1152=2^7*3^2`; neither is divisible by 7, so by Lagrange neither contains an element of order 7, and the group, containing a seven-cycle, preserves no nontrivial block system. (Independent route: a seven-cycle's orbits have sizes 1 and 7; a block of size 2 or 4 would be a union of orbit pieces or generate 7 pairwise-disjoint blocks inside 8 points — impossible either way.)
